WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.940
If our worst incident happens tomorrow,
will the policy pay?

00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:07.205
Our insurance has four
surprises at claim time.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.400
First, the application form.

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:12.785
Every policy starts with a questionnaire,

00:00:13.295 --> 00:00:16.045
and someone in IT ticks yes in good faith.

00:00:16.325 --> 00:00:17.315
Two years later,

00:00:17.565 --> 00:00:22.315
an adjuster finds one VPN that never
got multi-factor authentication.

00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:25.375
In many policies those
answers are warranties,

00:00:25.965 --> 00:00:29.395
so the insurer may reduce
the payout or void the cover.

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:31.967
Second, the exclusions.

00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:34.532
Since 31 March 2023,

00:00:34.922 --> 00:00:39.902
Lloyd's requires its syndicates to exclude
state-backed attacks from standalone cyber

00:00:39.902 --> 00:00:40.712
policies.

00:00:42.052 --> 00:00:46.122
Known vulnerabilities left unpatched
beyond a stated window are excluded too.

00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:50.342
So are regulatory fines and
contract penalties to customers.

00:00:50.727 --> 00:00:53.977
Each exclusion is exposure
the board carries itself.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.180
Third, the limits.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.290
The headline limit is not
the number that matters.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.600
Below it sit sub-limits
for forensics and legal,

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:06.380
and a waiting period before
business interruption cover starts.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.590
A serious incident uses up the
forensics cap in the first days.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:12.795
Everything above the cap is ours.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.340
Fourth, the phone call.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.940
Most policies require immediate notice,

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.650
and an approved panel of
forensics and legal firms.

00:01:21.985 --> 00:01:25.255
On day one, IT calls the
firm it already uses.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.900
Three days later, the insurer is told,
and declines those invoices.

00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:32.193
What good looks like.

00:01:32.478 --> 00:01:35.528
An evidence file behind
every questionnaire answer.

00:01:35.773 --> 00:01:39.223
An exclusion register,
with an amount next to each line.

00:01:39.513 --> 00:01:42.183
A limit test against a costed worst case.

00:01:42.508 --> 00:01:44.178
The insurer's hotline on line

00:01:44.568 --> 00:01:45.838
one of the contact list.

00:01:46.208 --> 00:01:50.658
NIS2 is clear that insurance does not
replace the required risk measures.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.740
A suggested first step.

00:01:54.025 --> 00:01:56.295
Ask three questions at the next renewal.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.300
What exactly is excluded?

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.330
How long is the waiting period?

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.220
Are the questionnaire
answers still true today?

00:02:04.717 --> 00:02:06.727
Insurance transfers some of the cost.

00:02:07.062 --> 00:02:09.352
It never transfers the responsibility.

